An example of cryptography online is email encryption. This is where emails are encrypted using public key encryption so only the intended recipient can read it.
Each email is encrypted with the public key of the recipient. Only the intended recipient can decrypt it, using their private key. Email encryption can be secured 
further by also encrypting the communication channels too. It is very important that emails are encrypted, because they often contain lots of personal identifiable
information that, if stolen, could be used for identity theft. For example; names, addresses, bank account numbers, login credentials, intellectual property and
patient health information.
There are several protocols for email encryption:
Pretty Good Privacy- Uses asymmetric key encryption, symmetric key encryption, hashing and data encryption to securely encrypt emails. It can be hard to use however.
Secure Sockets Layer (SSL)- The precurser to TSL, SSL begins with a 'handshake' between the two communicating devices to ensure their identities. SSL also signs the 
messages, to prove that they weren't tampered with during transit.
Advanced Encryption Standard (AES)- Symmetric encryption using very long keys, AES is the most common free encryption software, and is the standard for financial
companies. The length of the keys makes AES difficult to hack.

Source: https://www.microsoft.com/en-au/security/business/security-101/what-is-email-encryption
